Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Period live-action drama.

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A cramped fifth-floor walk-up in Milwaukee. MA, OLIVIA OLSON, a tired brown-haired woman with failing eyes, watches a game show beneath a faded afghan. Her younger son, THE NARRATOR, lean and ordinary in a pizza-parlor jacket, carries groceries inside.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Big brother was mine. I’m not talking about that stupid book we had to read in junior high. I’m talking about my real Big Brother.

EXT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

NICK OLSON, broad-shouldered, scarred at the chin and left eyebrow, reddish-brown hair uncombed, enters with THE NARRATOR. A weightlifter abandons a curl bench at Nick’s approach.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. His face looked like a puzzle with the pieces held together with sticky stuff from a glue gun.

Nick replaces the forty-pound bar with a heavier one and curls it easily. THE NARRATOR watches with pride.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

He was my brother. I knew he’d stand up for me no matter what.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

A CHILDHOOD NICK, twelve and already large, faces furious MRS. ENGEL beside her shattered kitchen window. CHILDHOOD NARRATOR and MARK CONNER hover behind him.

NICK

I was the one who did it.

MRS. ENGEL points toward the broken glass. Nick does not flinch.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Little Mark Conner kicked a ball right through old Mrs. Engel’s kitchen. Nick stepped right up and said he was the one who did it.

A week later, CHILDHOOD NICK slips cash into MA’S hand. She stares at it, astonished.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Somehow Nick gave her cash to pay for it. He never told Ma it was really Mark.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Adult NICK arrives in a brown suede jacket, keys from his burnt-orange Trans Am in hand. MA sleeps in the bedroom beyond.

NICK

What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?

THE NARRATOR glances toward the closed door.

NICK

You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn’t be sleeping in the middle of the day like that. That’s how old people go downhill.

THE NARRATOR nods, ashamed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Nick had a nice car, a good job at the metal factory, and an apartment on Werner Street. But he came over to take care of stuff, so it felt like he still lived here.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

THE NARRATOR walks his bike toward work. SIONNA, pale and slim, with short brown hair, light-blue eyes, a black V-neck shirt, jeans, blue tennis shoes, and a heavy backpack, trips on the cement and brushes his shoulder.

THE NARRATOR

Are you okay?

SIONNA brushes dust from her shirt.

SIONNA

Yes.

THE NARRATOR

I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA

Sionna.

THE NARRATOR studies her impossible blue eyes.

THE NARRATOR

Sionna? That’s different. I never heard of that.

SIONNA

My mom and dad named me after an Irish goddess. The River Shannon.

THE NARRATOR

Do you want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA

No thanks, I don’t eat pizza, but if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her number on a receipt. THE NARRATOR watches her walk away.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

It was like I knew her or something. Like I had seen her somewhere before.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

MA has pill bottles lined in a precise row across the counter. She searches frantically among them.

MA

I can’t find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

THE NARRATOR points to the pills.

THE NARRATOR

Ma, we don’t go to church anymore. Your pills are right in front of you.

MA looks at him with glassy, empty eyes, then suddenly sees the bottles.

MA

Oh, right. I am so sorry. I don’t know what got into me.

THE NARRATOR stares at her, frightened.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Packers football flickers over a smoky bar. NICK slaps THE NARRATOR on the back.

NICK

Hey, little brother. What’s shakin’?

THE NARRATOR tells him about MA’S confusion. Nick’s face reddens.

NICK

Why don’t you take her to the store or something? Holy Christ, do something with her before she turns into some demented old fart and we have to pay for a nursing home.

THE NARRATOR

I just needed to get away from her for a while.

NICK gives him a hard, dangerous look.

NICK

That’s more like it.

EXT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR leaves Joe’s and stops outside a grand old library. He looks back toward the bar, then enters.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

Under hanging lamps, THE NARRATOR fills out a library-card form. He walks through shelves of classic literature and pulls out Wuthering Heights.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Who needed school anyway when you could get the same thing for free in the library?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR reads Wuthering Heights in bed. The printed names Heathcliff and Catherine fill the page.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

I got the feeling these two really loved each other.

EXT. FOREST CLEARING - DREAM

THE NARRATOR, now dressed in a white linen shirt, embroidered red tunic, and brown hose, rides behind SIONNA on a dappled mare. Her hair falls long down her back.

They emerge into a sunlit field. He helps her down. They embrace and kiss.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

It was the finest hour of my life. I was falling in love with this woman.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR bolts upright, sweating.

THE NARRATOR

Hold!

He touches his chest, stunned.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

It was Sionna, but in a different place with old clothes on. What is happening?

INT. THE JAVA HUT - DAY

College students crowd tables with books and coffee. SIONNA waits at a small table in a loose green hooded top. THE NARRATOR sits across from her, clutching Wuthering Heights.

SIONNA

What are you reading right now?

THE NARRATOR

Wuthering Heights. It’s the story of this guy and girl and how they grew apart but were really in love with each other.

SIONNA

Yea, I know. I read it.

THE NARRATOR lowers his eyes, embarrassed. SIONNA smiles gently.

SIONNA

The environment of the book is like its people. Wild and unpredictable and infertile. The love Heathcliff has for Catherine never changes, but in the end that kind of love destroys him.

THE NARRATOR listens, awakened.

SIONNA

A love that never changes will destroy you.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Something inside of me was coming awake.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR, in a T-shirt tucked awkwardly into shorts, struggles through yoga among silent students. SIONNA moves smoothly on the next mat.

Later, he lies exhausted in Savasana. His face softens into peace.

SIONNA

You were really great at that.

THE NARRATOR

Are you kidding me?

SIONNA

Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what’s going on on your mat.

THE NARRATOR

Yeah, well, my practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA

That’s why they call it “practice.”

They smile at one another.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

NICK looms over THE NARRATOR. MA sits silent in her chair, frightened by the conflict.

NICK

Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

THE NARRATOR

I totally forgot. I’m sorry. I was with someone.

NICK

With someone? With who?

THE NARRATOR

I met a girl. We had a couple dates. That’s all.

NICK

Try looking at your priorities, Bro. You just might learn something.

Nick storms out. THE NARRATOR looks from the slammed door to MA.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Sometimes I really hate Nick inside.

INT. DOCTOR CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

MA grips her chest, dizzy and confused. DR. CARSON, a grave older physician, addresses THE NARRATOR.

DR. CARSON

Your mother’s blood pressure is dangerously high. I’m afraid of the risk of a stroke.

THE NARRATOR

Are you sure it’s that bad?

DR. CARSON

Take her directly to the emergency room. She needs to be admitted for observation.

THE NARRATOR looks toward MA, suddenly alone with the responsibility.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

MA sleeps under hospital blankets. THE NARRATOR slumps in a chair beside her bed and drifts off.

EXT. BATTLEFIELD - DREAM

THE NARRATOR, armored and bloodied, stands amid steel, mud, and dying soldiers. A wounded man gasps at his feet.

WOUNDED SOLDIER

Save her.

NICK appears through the chaos, carrying a long knife.

THE NARRATOR

Nick! Thank God you’re here!

NICK

That you are, little brother.

Nick drives the blade into THE NARRATOR’S chest.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR wakes gasping. MA dozes peacefully, unaware.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Nick had killed me. I felt betrayed by my closest family member.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

A blue weekly pill organizer sits on the coffee table. SIONNA helps MA place pills into morning and night compartments.

MA

You’re such a good boy. You and Nick are like a God-send to me.

NICK enters, sees SIONNA, and stops.

SIONNA offers her hand.

SIONNA

I’m Sionna. You must be Nick. Nice to meet you. I’ve heard so much about you.

NICK reluctantly shakes it.

NICK

Uh, yea. Likewise.

SIONNA serves peach pie. Nick refuses it.

NICK

Sugar makes me feel bloated and sick.

NICK eyes SIONNA.

NICK

So, I hear you’re a university lady.

SIONNA

That’s right.

NICK

What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA

I have a major in English and a minor in Philosophy. I’m thinking college professor.

NICK

Sometimes all that school just doesn’t pay off.

SIONNA calmly sets down her fork.

SIONNA

There are more females in college today than males. Diversity helps us learn. After all, we are all humans trying to live on this planet and make a difference.

NICK’S jaw tightens. THE NARRATOR hides a proud smile.

EXT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

NICK, THE NARRATOR, BOB, and SAMMY enter. Smoke, pool cues, and loud rock music fill the room.

At a pool table, NICK spots his former girlfriend LINDSEY with her new boyfriend, RAY.

NICK

Who’s she with? Who’s that asshole?

THE NARRATOR

Why does it matter, Bro? You broke up with her.

NICK stalks toward LINDSEY. THE NARRATOR follows.

NICK

Where did you meet this one? We keep our ears out, if you know what I mean.

RAY steps beside LINDSEY.

RAY

What the fuck is wrong with you?

NICK clenches his fists. THE NARRATOR moves directly before him.

THE NARRATOR

Nick, listen. You don’t have any problem with this guy. Leave it, man.

NICK

Keep out of it. You’re going to be next if you don’t get out of my face.

THE NARRATOR trembles but stays.

THE NARRATOR

Stop it! No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick swings. THE NARRATOR’S head snaps aside. JOE and his bouncer seize Nick as LINDSEY pulls RAY toward the exit.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR runs through the freezing dark, breath tearing in his throat.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

SIONNA holds THE NARRATOR on her green suede sofa. He has a reddening bruise on his face.

SIONNA

Your brother tried to hit you.

THE NARRATOR

He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick completely.

SIONNA

No one who really cares about you does that.

THE NARRATOR rests his head in her lap.

THE NARRATOR

The first time I met you, I felt so comfortable. It was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA kisses the top of his head, tears in her eyes.

SIONNA

Me too, honey. Me too.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

The coffeepot has boiled dry. THE NARRATOR throws it into the sink with an oven mitt. MA cries in her chair.

MA

I can’t remember anything anymore. I’m no good for anything.

THE NARRATOR kneels, taking her hands.

THE NARRATOR

You’re great, Ma. I love you.

MA

Nick doesn’t love me. He never comes to see me anymore.

THE NARRATOR looks toward the window, exhausted.

THE NARRATOR

I’ll check on him. I’ll make sure everything is okay.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

A once-proud apartment is buried in fast-food containers, dirty clothes, and papers. NICK opens the door on its chain, unshaven and furious.

NICK

What do YOU want?

THE NARRATOR

Ma is worried about you. I think it’s about time we talked this out.

NICK lets him in.

NICK

You’ve ruined my life. People are talkin’ shit about me. They’re saying my little brother tried to kick my ass.

THE NARRATOR

I didn’t do anything, man. I just tried to stop you.

NICK

You’re some hot-shit college kid with a hoity-toity girlfriend, and you know everything.

THE NARRATOR

I hardly know anything. That’s the thing about going to school. You find out how much there is out there that you don’t know.

NICK studies him, then points a warning finger.

NICK

I’ll let it slide this time. But don’t you ever, EVER interrupt me in public again.

THE NARRATOR

Will you see Ma?

NICK

Yeah. Tell her I’ve been sick or somethin’.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

MA, SIONNA, and THE NARRATOR share dinner. NICK enters carrying takeout. He gently kisses MA’S cheek.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Nick started taking Ma out, bringing food, laying out her pills. It was too much. You don’t go from scary mother to pet your Mom’s head and escort her to church.

MA beams at Nick. THE NARRATOR watches, uneasy.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

The apartment is empty. MA’S purse and coat are gone. THE NARRATOR notices the blinking answering machine.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)

Mr. Olson, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff’s Department. We need to talk to you regarding your family.

PATRICIA OWENS (V.O.)

This is Patricia Owens, one of the nurses at Frodo Hospital, with an update on your family members.

THE NARRATOR goes pale, then races out.

INT. MILWAUKEE COUNTY SHERIFF'S STATION - DAY

OFFICER DUPUIS, a heavyset man with graying hair and a thin mustache, sits opposite THE NARRATOR.

OFFICER DUPUIS

There has been a serious accident involving your brother and your mother.

THE NARRATOR grips a cup of water.

OFFICER DUPUIS

Last night they were hit broadside at Werner and Lowe. Your brother is in serious condition.

THE NARRATOR’S breath catches.

OFFICER DUPUIS

Your mother passed away at the scene.

The cup slips from THE NARRATOR’S shaking hand.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

MA’S chair sits empty beneath the afghan. THE NARRATOR collapses before it, sobbing like a child. SIONNA enters and holds him.

THE NARRATOR

He fucking killed my mom.

SIONNA

Just let it out. We’ll figure it all out. Don’t worry.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

NICK lies bruised and yellow beneath tubes, his leg immobilized and a chest tube draining into a plastic box. THE NARRATOR stands at his bedside.

NICK

Hey, bro.

THE NARRATOR

They took care of it. The mass was nice. She’s at peace now.

NICK’S bloodshot eyes fill.

NICK

I guess I really fucked up, man. Can’t believe Ma’s gone.

THE NARRATOR

Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK stares at him.

THE NARRATOR

Think about it.

He turns and leaves Nick trapped in the bed.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

SIONNA cooks in the kitchen. NICK stands in the living room doorway, larger than the room, agitated and cold.

NICK

If it wasn’t for you messing around with my brother, everything would be cool. Ma would be alive right now.

SIONNA

What makes you think I’m responsible for you going to jail?

NICK advances. SIONNA backs into the wall.

NICK

You got two things to do. Come up with some money, and get the fuck away from my brother.

SIONNA

Nick, calm down. This really has nothing to do with me.

Nick clamps a hand around her throat and flicks open a switchblade.

NICK

Break it off with him tonight or else.

The front door bursts open. THE NARRATOR sees Nick holding SIONNA against the wall.

THE NARRATOR

Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

He grabs Nick’s arm. In the struggle, the knife plunges into SIONNA’S upper chest. Blood sprays across her shirt.

NICK shoves THE NARRATOR away and flees.

THE NARRATOR catches SIONNA as she sinks to the floor. He rips off his flannel shirt and presses both hands against the wound.

THE NARRATOR

Stay with me. Hurry up. Hurry up.

Sirens rise outside.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

Months later. SIONNA sits blankly in a rocking chair, her shoulder scar hidden beneath a loose sleeve. THE NARRATOR kneels in front of her.

THE NARRATOR

You know I love you, right?

SIONNA

I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

THE NARRATOR

You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA’S blue eyes fill.

SIONNA

As long as there is any doubt, I can’t go on with you. Maybe I need to go to Mom and Dad’s for a while.

THE NARRATOR’S face crumples.

THE NARRATOR

Please stay.

SIONNA slowly walks away to the bedroom. He remains alone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

She was my life guide. I relied on her to fill up my consciousness. My exit from that house was a free fall into the black, endless void of space.

EXT. PIKE PLACE MARKET - DAY

Seven years later. Seattle rain glazes the streets. THE NARRATOR, older, healthier, and sharply dressed, walks past market stalls toward a storefront sign: TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Thanks to my old boss Tony, I had done pretty well for myself. I had my own chain of pizzerias in the Northwest.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles cast low light over meditators. THE NARRATOR sits cross-legged, eyes closed.

INSTRUCTOR MAYA

Tonight, we practice gratefulness.

THE NARRATOR opens his eyes, troubled.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

I could identify sorrow for Nick. Feelings of a long-ago bond that DNA demands.

INT. THE NARRATOR'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR wakes from a dream beside BOZO, an orange-and-white cat. He writes urgently in a notebook.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Holding onto resentment kept me in my own prison. I felt like I finally had the key to unlock it.

He opens his laptop and books a flight to Milwaukee.

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

THE NARRATOR stands before MA’S grave marker. Golden mums fill its small metal vase.

NARRATOR (V.O.)

Mother: Olivia Anne Olsen. I have fought the good fight. I have finished the race. I have kept the faith.

THE NARRATOR touches the stone.

THE NARRATOR

Yes, you did, Ma.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE VISITING ROOM - DAY

Behind thick Plexiglas, NICK appears in prison clothes. He is thin now, his hair gray at the temples, his old scars softened by age. THE NARRATOR lifts a yellow phone.

NICK

Hi, bro. Glad you came.

THE NARRATOR takes the opposite phone.

THE NARRATOR

Hi, Nick. Good to see you finally.

NICK

It’s not a cakewalk. But I got my routine down. Maybe two more years before parole.

THE NARRATOR breathes deeply.

THE NARRATOR

I’m here to tell you some things. I would appreciate it if you would just listen.

NICK

Yeah. Okay, man.

THE NARRATOR

Even though you are my brother and we have been through a lot of shit together, I am no longer interested in seeing you or hearing anything you have to say to me.

Nick lowers his eyes.

THE NARRATOR

You killed my mother in a drunk driving accident. You held me down my entire life. But mostly, you tried to kill the love of my life, which drove her away.

NICK’S eyes redden.

THE NARRATOR

Frankly, you’re an asshole.

NICK receives the words without protest.

THE NARRATOR

But this is the most important bit. I forgive you.

Nick looks up.

THE NARRATOR

I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

Tears stream down Nick’s face.

NICK

I’m so sorry. You’re so right, man. I did do all that shit.

THE NARRATOR

After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother. Do you hear me?

NICK nods through tears.

NICK

I hear you, man. I love you too. What are you doing now?

THE NARRATOR allows himself a small smile.

THE NARRATOR

I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We’ll be expanding. You always said I couldn’t do it. I did it, Nick. I really did it.

NICK smiles weakly.

NICK

President Olson. That’s fucking amazing. I am so proud of you, man.

A CORRECTIONAL OFFICER approaches Nick.

CORRECTIONAL OFFICER

Time’s up.

NICK

Bye.

THE NARRATOR hangs up the phone and watches Nick disappear down the institutional hallway.

EXT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

THE NARRATOR walks toward his gate carrying tea and mixed nuts. He looks lighter than he has in years.

NICK (V.O.)

President Olson.

THE NARRATOR looks at his boarding pass.

THE NARRATOR

That’s me. President Peter Olson.

He joins the boarding line as sunlight breaks through the clouds.

FADE OUT.
